mpc2000xl: a key-on starts a stereo pair, and a recorded voice is not played

Replaces the previous 0055 wholesale; it kept everything that patch got right
and adds the two findings that finish the sampling path. Everything below was
measured with scripts/diagnostics/run-sample-rig.sh, which drives the panel
from inside the machine and pushes a known 440 Hz LEFT / 660 Hz RIGHT probe
into RECORD IN while :speaker is captured. The SAMPLE screen was confirmed to
read Mode:STEREO Monitor:L/R from an exported LCD frame, not assumed.

A KEY-ON STARTS A STEREO PAIR, NOT ONE VOICE. This is why only the right half
of anything stereo could be heard - the input monitor, an auditioned take, and
every stereo pad sample alike. The firmware never issues two key-ons for a
stereo sound. It selects and programs the left voice, selects and programs the
right voice, and strobes bit 8 once:

  * the monitor routine at 0x49308 copies a template into two blocks, programs
    voice 0x15 with the LEFT record region 0x010000-0x011140 and a hard-left
    mixer (L=0x80 R=0x00) and voice 0x17 with the RIGHT region
    0x011140-0x012280 and a hard-right mixer, then does
        in al,0x88 / and al,0x7f / mov ah,1 / out 0x88,ax
    at 0x493cc with NO voice select in front of it. Both voice numbers are
    literals - push byte 0x15, push byte 0x17 - and the register programmer it
    calls at 0x3e5fc contains no write to the control port at all;
  * arming a take programs voice 0x00 and voice 0x10 the same way and strobes;
  * an ordinary stereo pad hit programs voices 2 and 4 - and 6 and 8 for the
    next hit - and strobes once through the playback module's key-on at
    0x3e597.

Three pairs at three different distances, 2 apart, 2 apart and 16 apart, from
an allocator that hands voices out in order, so the partner cannot be computed
from the voice number. What is invariant across all six key-ons in a full
recorded session is that the partner is the voice selected immediately before
the current one. So the chip latches selected voices and the key-on strobe
starts them together and empties the latch, which is the only way a pair can
start on the same sample when one register write cannot name two voices. The
latch being emptied is what keeps a mono voice mono. That the latch is two
deep and that the strobe clears it is inference fitted to the measurements;
that one strobe must start two voices is not - the firmware issues no second
one, and voice 0x15 is otherwise programmed perfectly and never heard.

A VOICE BEING RECORDED INTO IS OWNED BY THE TRANSFER, NOT BY PLAYBACK. A voice
has one address counter, and while a record transfer is running the DMA is
what advances it - one step per word written, which is what the firmware times
the take by. Playing the voice at the same time advances it a second time.
This was already happening to the right half of every stereo take, because the
record transfer's key-on landed on voice 0x10: the trace shows posR climbing
at exactly twice pos0, 41102 against 20559 and 85215 against 42609, for the
whole take. Keying the left half too - which the pair key-on correctly does -
extended it to voice 0, and that is the counter the take-length poll reads, so
the take came out a fraction of the length asked for. Not playing a voice the
transfer owns fixes both, and it is also why the machine monitors through a
separate pair of voices aimed at the very same two regions: voices 0 and 0x10
are busy being written and cannot play them back.

Measured on :speaker per stage, peak of a single-bin DFT per 0.25 s window,
before and after (L@440 L@660 R@440 R@660, and the worse of the two
input-to-output separation ratios):

    MONITOR   before 0.0000 0.0000 0.0017 0.0741    0.0x
              after  0.0889 0.0015 0.0018 0.0741   48.4x
    RECORD    before 0.0000 0.0000 0.0024 0.0018    0.0x
              after  0.0871 0.0014 0.0009 0.0583   40.3x
    PLAYBACK  before 0.0000 0.0000 0.0041 0.0083    0.0x
              after  0.1837 0.0045 0.0022 0.1236   27.6x
    PAD       before 0.0000 0.0000 0.0085 0.0088    0.0x
              after  0.2789 0.0035 0.0022 0.1355   39.2x

The left output carried nothing at all before. It now carries the left input
and only the left input, and the right output the right input, at every stage.
The level rise on PLAYBACK and PAD is the second fix: the right half of the
take was previously written at every other address.

Everything the previous 0055 established is unchanged and still verified by
the same rig - the record region is a circular buffer that wraps rather than
stopping, the send destination is three bits and not four, and the record
transfer's key-on is the machine keying its own monitor and must not be
suppressed.

STILL OPEN. The ADC ring occasionally drains, depth 30 dipping to 2 in about
one report in seven, which is the residual level-meter flicker.

The diagnostics that found all this are included: MAME_MPC_SAMPLE_DEBUG for
the running commentary and a per-second census of which voices are audible,
and MAME_MPC_DSP_TRACE=<path> for a complete journal of the guest's register
traffic, armed the first time the sampler is enabled. The pairing rule is not
visible without that journal.

